NAME
    sh - the AdventOS shell

SYNOPSIS
    sh                          interactive prompt
    sh FILE.sh                  run FILE.sh in a fresh shell

DESCRIPTION
    The interactive shell. Reads commands from stdin (when
    interactive) or from a script file, forks+execs each external
    command, and handles pipelines, redirection, jobs, env vars,
    and history.

LINE EDITING (sessions 49 + 84)
    Backspace         erase char before cursor
    Left / Right      move cursor (also Ctrl-B / Ctrl-F)
    Home  / Ctrl-A    start of line
    End   / Ctrl-E    end of line
    Up / Down         walk the command history
    Tab               complete a filename from the cwd
    Ctrl-W            delete the word before cursor
    Ctrl-U            delete from start of line to cursor
    Ctrl-K            delete from cursor to end of line
    Ctrl-C            discard the current line

PIPELINES + REDIRECTION
    cmd1 | cmd2 | ... [> outfile]
      Each stage is a separate fork()+exec(); | wires stdin/stdout
      via SYS_PIPE+SYS_DUP2; > opens an in-RAM tmpfs file you can
      later cat back.

    cmd1 |> cmd2 |> ...
      Structured pipeline (session 81). Every stage is run with
      --advjson; producers emit JSONL records and consumers parse
      JSONL. See `man pluck`, `man where`, `man count`, and
      docs/69-structured-pipelines.md.

JOBS
    cmd &                 run in the background
    jobs                  list active job table
    fg %N                 bring job N to foreground
    bg %N                 resume job N in the background

ENV VARS (session 49)
    export NAME=value     set; $NAME expands in future commands
    unset NAME            remove
    env                   list all current env vars

SCRIPTS
    sh FILE.sh            run FILE.sh in a fresh shell (env not shared)
    source FILE.sh        run FILE.sh in THIS shell (env persists)

EXIT
    exit [CODE]           exit (default code: 0)

SEE ALSO
    init(1), ed(1), vi(1)
